there are more than fifteen hundred  mosques across the uk  and for more than two million muslims  currently living in britain it's a place  to pray and promote peace but promoting  peace is hard in the face of hate and  for this community it's on the rise we  found that almost half of mosques which  were surveyed  were subject to racially motivated as  religiously motivated attacks within the  last three years and that's because of a  backdrop of islamophobic narratives  which are being peddled  throughout wider society  according to data from the muslim  engagement and development group  otherwise known as mend  42 of mosques and islamic institutions  have experienced religiously motivated  attacks in the last three years  and 35 percent experience a religiously  motivated attack at least once a year  with racially and religiously aggravated  offences increasing by 15 percent in the  last year  worrying but not surprising especially  for those that attend this mosque at  finsbury park  where a terrorist drove into worshippers  in  2017  killing makaram ali and injuring 11  others  our communities still feel the fears and  the intimidation and also you know they  expect probably an attack anytime  because what's happened just only it's  not one-off  attack on the community  we fear that  probably other attacks might might take  place unless we do something about  islamophobia in a statement the  government says we take a zero tolerance  approach to all forms of hate crime  we're taking steps to safeguard places  of worship with a grant of over  24.5 million pounds available this year  men say that only a third of mosques  that applied for the government place of  worship fund actually received it  it's asking for it to be more accessible  so that mosques that are at risk can be  better secured and worshipers can pray  in peace  shaman freeman powell sky news
 

